export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>You are looking at <em>Dragon's Lair</em>, an arcade game released in 1983. This game is basically an interactive movie, stitching various sequences together according to the player's inputs. If you press the right button, you move to the next sequence; otherwise, it's game over. It is a series of events that demands you to time your button presses in quick successions. In other words: quick time events (QTEs).</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/befe4f2de284ac858e2d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/befe4f2de284ac858e2d.webp\" width=\"563\" height=\"316\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/55e9018fead17fb1e0e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/55e9018fead17fb1e0e9.webp\" width=\"553\" height=\"311\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><em>Shenmue </em>(1999) is the progenitor of modern QTEs. Yu Suzuki, the game's creator, described implemented such a system in hopes of engendering \"a fusion of gameplay and movie.\" During regular gameplay, the player could only act within the constraints of the controls, so to have more complex and cinematic actions, Mr. Suzuki added timing challenges, which allowed the player character to perform actions that would be otherwise impossible.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In 2005, QTEs exploded in popularity thanks to the release of <em>God of War</em> and <em>Resident Evil 4</em>. Although they both implemented QTEs, their goals with the system differed drastically.</p>\n<p><em>God of War</em> aimed to deliver large-scale spectacle, and like <em>Shenmue</em>, the game valued its cinematic quality. <em>Resident Evil</em> <em>4, </em>on the other hand, leaned toward the challenging aspect of QTEs. To make the moment-to-moment gameplay tense even in the absence of enemies, the game constantly puts players on edge with scripted threats that can result in a fail state.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/8fa99b38b51ac92e145f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/8fa99b38b51ac92e145f.webp\" width=\"547\" height=\"305\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/bb6e46dfb772b995ddb4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/bb6e46dfb772b995ddb4.webp\" width=\"547\" height=\"304\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/90227904d93c7f24dcd2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/90227904d93c7f24dcd2.webp\" width=\"554\" height=\"310\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/62cd6ceaa7e823982713.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/62cd6ceaa7e823982713.webp\" width=\"554\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>In the wake of <em>God of War</em> and <em>Resident Evil 4</em>, QTEs became widespread in the games industry. As more games adopted this mechanic, it led to some less than ideal uses.</p>\n<p>If you are part of the gaming discourse, you must have stumbled across this meme: \"Press F to pay respect.\" This hilarious interaction came from an emotional scene <em>Call of Duty: Advanced Warfare </em>where soldiers are gathering up at a funeral.</p>\n<p>Another equally questionable case is from <em>Ace Combat: Assault Horizon, </em>where in one scene you have to press triangle to raise your hand.</p>\n<p>Of course, no developers set out to make a bad game, so it's hard to say: \"They were out of their minds!\" A more charitable interpretation is they thought these sequences would turn out funny, like we eventually think they are. Is that really what happened behind the scenes? We may never know. Does it matter in the grand scheme of things? Not necessarily.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Mechanically, QTEs can induce frustration if the button prompts aren't consistent enough. One example we can turn to is <em>Resident Evil 5</em>. Similar to its predecessor, <em>Resident Evil 5</em> has a lot of QTEs during gameplay to catch you off-guard. But, instead of fixed actions to the prompts like the fourth game did, <em>Resident Evil 5</em> issues random buttons and combinations of buttons to supposedly increase the stakes. This change didn't sit well with players, as they found it make the QTEs too arbitrary.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0e928ded5a488d5b56a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0e928ded5a488d5b56a.webp\" width=\"527\" height=\"591\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/6da962c7256324355a77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/6da962c7256324355a77.webp\" width=\"580\" height=\"325\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Looking back at <em>Shenmue </em>and <em>God of War</em> can help us glean some useful tips for nailing QTEs. The first thing is going to be consistency, specifically in accordance with the regular control scheme. If a QTE asks the player to dodge, it should ask for the regular dodge button to be pressed.</p>\n<p>The prompt placement on the screen is also very important. <em>God of War 3 </em>emulates the layout of the Playstation controller, so the player can tell which button to press just by the position of the prompt.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>QTEs are still a staple in game design today, but their prevalence has diminished. An example is <em>Marvel's</em> <em>Spider-Man</em>, which has an option to remove QTEs from all of its scripted sequences. With this option, the game gets to maintain its cinematic moments and becomes more accessible. After all, it's very difficult to craft interactive set-pieces without QTEs.</p>\n<p><em>God of War (2018)</em>, the continuation of a series known for QTEs, significantly toned down the use of scripted behavior. While button prompts still pop up from time to time, they now have more lenient timings, and almost never result in a game over screen. Some people aren't bothered by this change, some are because they think it drains all the tension from these moments.</p>\n<p>Ultimate, it's up to personal preferences, so we would like to end this article with a question: <strong>What do you think about QTEs?</strong> Not just in <em>God of War</em>, but in all of video games. Do you like them or not? Let us know because we are very curious about that.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b9625efd146d0dd3db06.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b9625efd146d0dd3db06.webp\" width=\"382\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/d52802c7d4c9d1bc99ba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/d52802c7d4c9d1bc99ba.webp\" width=\"532\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:289px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
